# Dart class member order

Declare class members in this order. Do not put the constructor above fields.

1. **Static** constants / fields / methods (if any)
2. **Instance fields** (including `late` / `final` / private)
3. **Getters / props** (e.g. `logName`, `valueStream`, Equatable `props`)
4. **Constructors** (generative, then named / factory)
5. **`dispose` / `close`** (last among methods)
6. **Public methods**
7. **Private methods**

```dart
// ✅ GOOD
class CommentsRepositoryImpl {
  final ApiClient _apiClient;
  final LocalStore _localStore;
  final _sessions = <int, _CommentsSession>{};

  UserId? get activeUserId => _activeUserId;

  CommentsRepositoryImpl(this._apiClient, this._localStore, ObserveMyUserUseCase observeMyUser) {
    // ...
  }

  Future<void> dispose() async { /* ... */ }

  ValueStream<ValueState<Comments>> commentsForPost(int postId) => /* ... */;

  Future<void> _clearSessions() async { /* ... */ }
}

// ❌ BAD — constructor before fields
class CommentsRepositoryImpl {
  CommentsRepositoryImpl(this._apiClient, this._localStore) { /* ... */ }

  final ApiClient _apiClient;
  final LocalStore _localStore;
}
```

Notes:

- Initializing formal parameters (`this._apiClient`) are fine; the **field declarations** still sit above the constructor body.
- `late final` fields that are assigned in the constructor are still **declared** above it.
- Prefer matching this order in new code and when touching a class; do not mass-reorder unrelated files in the same change.
